1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:06,240
The Great Enu War, 1932.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:08,320
November 4th, 1932. In Western

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:10,719
Australia, soldiers wait beside a dam

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:12,400
with machine guns. Ahead of them, around

5
00:00:10,719 --> 00:00:14,559
a thousand emus are approaching the

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:16,960
water. The birds have finally gathered

7
00:00:14,559 --> 00:00:20,880
in one place. The soldiers let them come

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:23,439
closer, then open fire. A few emus fall,

9
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:25,760
then a machine gun jams. The rest

10
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:27,680
scatter across the open land. It was one

11
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:30,960
of their best chances to stop a whole

12
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:32,640
flock. Almost every bird escaped. How

13
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:35,280
did Australia end up sending machine

14
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:38,320
guns against emuse? To understand that,

15
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:40,160
go back 2 days. On November 2nd, a small

16
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:42,399
army unit arrives in the wheat growing

17
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:44,239
district near Campion. The men have two

18
00:00:42,399 --> 00:00:46,320
machine guns and orders to help local

19
00:00:44,239 --> 00:00:47,840
farmers get rid of the birds. Those

20
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:50,719
farmers have been fighting to keep their

21
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:52,559
farms alive. After the First World War,

22
00:00:50,719 --> 00:00:54,800
many former soldiers had settled on land

23
00:00:52,559 --> 00:00:57,280
in Western Australia. Some of that land

24
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,039
was hard to farm even in good years. In

25
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:01,600
parts of the district, settlers had

26
00:00:59,039 --> 00:01:03,840
already abandoned their farms. Then the

27
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:06,000
Great Depression struck. Wheat prices

28
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:08,400
collapsed. Drought made everything

29
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,720
worse. For the families who stayed, the

30
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,479
harvest was their livelihood. A lost

31
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:15,200
field of wheat could mean a year of work

32
00:01:12,479 --> 00:01:17,759
with nothing to show for it. And now the

33
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:19,920
emus were arriving. They were huge,

34
00:01:17,759 --> 00:01:21,920
flightless birds, nearly as tall as a

35
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:23,920
person, with powerful legs built for

36
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,600
running. Thousands were moving through

37
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:28,080
the farming districts looking for food

38
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:30,080
and water. To the birds, the wheat

39
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:32,000
fields were an easy meal. To the

40
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:34,640
farmers, they were crops that had taken

41
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,720
months of work to grow. The emus ate the

42
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:38,400
grain and trampled what they didn't eat.

43
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:40,320
They damaged fences as they moved

44
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:43,040
through, leaving openings for rabbits to

45
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:45,360
enter and do even more harm. The farmers

46
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:47,439
couldn't keep them out, so they turned

47
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:49,200
to the government for help. The defense

48
00:01:47,439 --> 00:01:51,439
minister agreed to send soldiers with

49
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:54,159
machine guns. A cameraman came along to

50
00:01:51,439 --> 00:01:56,720
record the operation. On paper, the plan

51
00:01:54,159 --> 00:01:59,439
seemed simple. Find a large flock. Bring

52
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:01,040
the guns within range. Open fire. But

53
00:01:59,439 --> 00:02:03,360
the soldiers discovered the problem

54
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:06,079
almost as soon as they arrived. On their

55
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:07,680
first day, they spotted a group of emus.

56
00:02:06,079 --> 00:02:10,239
Farmers tried to herd the birds toward

57
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:12,879
the waiting guns. Instead, the flock

58
00:02:10,239 --> 00:02:15,040
broke apart. Some birds ran one way,

59
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:16,720
others ran another. By the time the

60
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:18,959
soldiers could shoot, there were only

61
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:21,440
small groups moving across the fields.

62
00:02:18,959 --> 00:02:23,680
Only a few were killed. The soldiers had

63
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:25,440
weapons made to deliver sustained fire,

64
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:27,760
but that meant little when the targets

65
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:29,920
kept scattering, and then came the

66
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:31,680
opportunity at the dam. Around a

67
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:33,920
thousand emus were gathered near the

68
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:36,239
water. This time, the soldiers waited

69
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:38,239
until they were close before firing. But

70
00:02:36,239 --> 00:02:41,440
after roughly a dozen birds were killed,

71
00:02:38,239 --> 00:02:43,440
a gun jammed. The flock escaped. The men

72
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:45,920
had found the large target they needed

73
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:48,000
and lost it. So, they tried something

74
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:49,680
different. They put a machine gun on the

75
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,360
back of a truck. If they couldn't get

76
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:53,280
the birds to come to the guns, they

77
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:56,000
could take the gun after the birds.

78
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:58,160
There was just one problem. Emus can run

79
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,560
fast. The truck couldn't keep up over

80
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:02,720
the rough ground. It jolted so badly

81
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:04,560
that the gunner struggled to aim. A

82
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:06,400
machine gun was now bouncing across the

83
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:09,360
countryside, chasing birds it could

84
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:11,280
barely reach. The soldiers kept trying,

85
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:13,680
but each encounter ended much the same

86
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:15,920
way. A burst of gunfire, birds

87
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:18,159
scattering, and more emus disappearing

88
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:20,080
over the fields. Their commander, Major

89
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:22,879
Meredith, compared the bird's ability to

90
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:25,280
withstand gunfire to that of tanks. Back

91
00:03:22,879 --> 00:03:27,440
in Canra, the operation was attracting a

92
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:29,599
different kind of attention. Politicians

93
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:31,440
questioned the campaign. One joked that

94
00:03:29,599 --> 00:03:33,599
if anyone deserved medals, it was the

95
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:35,280
emuse. A few days after the failed

96
00:03:33,599 --> 00:03:37,840
ambush, the government recalled the

97
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:40,239
soldiers. In Canberra, the shooting was

98
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:42,400
over. In the farming district, the birds

99
00:03:40,239 --> 00:03:44,879
were still eating wheat. The soldiers

100
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:47,280
had gone, but the damage hadn't stopped.

101
00:03:44,879 --> 00:03:49,680
Fences were still being broken. Crops

102
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:51,840
were still at risk. The birds were still

103
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:53,760
moving through the district. The farmers

104
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:56,080
asked for help again, and the government

105
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:58,319
agreed to another attempt. So, the

106
00:03:56,080 --> 00:04:00,879
soldiers returned with their guns. This

107
00:03:58,319 --> 00:04:03,280
time, they killed more emus. But the

108
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:05,200
work dragged on for weeks. The birds

109
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:06,720
were spread across too much country, and

110
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:08,799
the soldiers had to find them before

111
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:10,799
they could fire. Even when the guns were

112
00:04:08,799 --> 00:04:13,120
successful, another group of emus could

113
00:04:10,799 --> 00:04:15,360
turn up somewhere else. By December,

114
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:17,759
hundreds of birds had been killed. Even

115
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:20,000
so, thousands remained in the district.

116
00:04:17,759 --> 00:04:22,400
The shooting hadn't stopped the damage.

117
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,800
The army ended its campaign. For the

118
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:26,479
farmers, there was no dramatic finish.

119
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,560
They still needed ways to protect their

120
00:04:26,479 --> 00:04:30,560
fields. In the years that followed,

121
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,160
people continued killing emus for bounty

122
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,320
payments. While fences remained

123
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:36,800
important for protecting crops, for the

124
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:38,960
birds, the campaign had been deadly.

125
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:40,800
Many had been shot, but thousands

126
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:42,720
remained across the region. Australia

127
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:44,479
had sent soldiers into farmland with

128
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:46,560
machine guns to stop the birds from

129
00:04:44,479 --> 00:04:49,600
destroying the wheat. The soldiers had

130
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:51,919
fired, chased, withdrawn, and returned.

131
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:54,560
The farmers still needed protection. The

132
00:04:51,919 --> 00:04:56,400
emus had suffered heavy losses. Yet

133
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:58,320
across the wheat growing district, the

134
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:00,720
birds continued searching for food and

135
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:04,600
water. The soldiers were gone, and the

136
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:04,600
emus kept coming.

